HEVCTest - the Raspberry Pi 4's HEVC block, from RISC OS
=======================================================

The Pi 4 has an HEVC (H.265) decoder block of its own, separate from the
VideoCore's H.264 decoder. Linux drives it with Raspberry Pi's "rpivid"
driver. hevcdec is that driver's command builder (rpivid_h265.c, as it is)
around a small RISC OS layer: the block's registers mapped and used in
SVC mode, its clock at its maximum, contiguous memory from Physical
Memory Pools, and the two phases' completion polled.

The block is a stateless decoder: it's given each picture's parameters
and slices, already parsed. For this test they were parsed on a Linux
machine by FFmpeg's HEVC decoder and saved with the slices in each clip's
"trace", with checksums of the picture FFmpeg decoded. HEVCTest gives
the block every picture as the trace says and checks the block's picture
against FFmpeg's.

The clips (in clips; each has its MP4 file, mp4, and its trace):
  small   352x288, 20 pictures, B frames, wavefront (WPP) entry points
  slices  416x240, 12 pictures, four slices a picture
  nowpp   416x240, 12 pictures, no WPP, scaling lists
  odd     426x240, 8 pictures (coded 432 wide, cropped)
  hd      1920x1080, 60 pictures, B frames, WPP
  and 10-bit (new in 0.1.8):
  small10, slices10, odd10   as small, slices and odd, 10-bit
  crop10  416x240 cropped to 368x228 from 16,8 (the left edge mid-word)
  hd10    1920x1080, 60 pictures, 10-bit
  and 4K (new in 0.1.8): uhd and uhd10, 3840x2160, 30 pictures, 8-bit and
  10-bit

Running it (please send back Result, ResultT, ResultP, ResultK and ResultC):
  Test     Every clip but the 4K ones, every picture checked. Writes
           Result.
  Speed    The 1080p clips (8-bit, 10-bit) without checks: pictures a
           second. Writes ResultT.
  Convert  (0.1.9) The 1080p and 4K clips' third picture converted 30
           times each way, timed: which order of copying is quickest on
           the Pi. Writes ResultC.
  UHD      The 4K clips (8-bit, 10-bit): every picture checked, then
           timed, then timed pipelined. Writes ResultK. Needs about 100
           MB for hevctest and about 170 MB for the block's frames.
  Verbose  The first three pictures of the small clip with the
           decoder's log, if Test fails. Writes ResultV.
  Pipelined Every clip with each picture given to the block without
           waiting (checked while it decodes the next), then the 1080p
           clip timed that way and with four pictures ahead. Writes
           ResultP: compare its pictures a second with Speed's.
  Uncached The 1080p clip with the output frames not cacheable, as
           before 0.1.4, to compare. Writes ResultU.
  NoHW     hevctest starts and stops without touching the HEVC block
           (adds a line to ResultN): for the "EMT trap" below.
  Repeat   hevctest run straight after itself 13 times (the small
           clip ten times, the 1080p clip three). Writes ResultR.
  Dump     Saves the pictures decoded (the first three of small,
           twice, and all of odd) to DumpSmall, DumpSmall2 and DumpOdd,
           to compare with FFmpeg's pixel by pixel, if Test fails.
           Writes ResultD.

0.1 on a Pi 4: the block decoded every picture, and the clip with
scaling lists (nowpp) came out exactly right; the clips without them
didn't, differently each run. 0.1.1 showed why: the block keeps and uses
its scaling factors even for streams without scaling lists, and they
weren't being loaded. Given flat ones (-s), every picture of every clip
was right. 0.1.2's hevcdec always loads them, and every picture of
every clip was right. 0.1.3 looks into hevctest sometimes stopping at
once ("EMT trap") when run straight after itself: every buffer the block
uses now has a guard area after it, and hevctest says whether the block
wrote past any; Repeat runs it back to back. 0.1.3 on a Pi 4: all
right, nothing written past any buffer, Repeat fine. But converting a
1080p picture from the block's format took 29 ms, reading memory that
isn't cacheable. 0.1.4 makes the output frames cacheable (cleaned and
invalidated before each is read) and converts with NEON; Test's 1080p
line "converting them" should be much lower than Uncached's. 0.1.4: 14 cs
against 174 (2.3 ms a 1080p picture), Uncached 40 cs; all right.

The EMT trap: hevctest sometimes stops at once ("Fatal signal received:
EMT trap") when started from the Filer straight after another run, but
never within one Obey file (Repeat). It happens in UnixLib opening the
output file, before the HEVC block is used. To find out whose it is,
please try, starting each straight after the one before finishes:
  1  NoHW, NoHW, NoHW, ...       (no HEVC block at all)
  2  Speed, then NoHW            (the block, then none)
  3  Speed, then Speed
and photograph the screen if it stops: 0.1.5 now prints RISC OS's own
error and address first ("hevctest: signal ...").

That turned out to be UnixLib's and ARMEABISupport's (NoHW, which never
touches the HEVC block, does it too), so it's reported to them. Until
it's fixed, wait a moment before starting the next Obey file.

0.1.9 (hevcdec 0.1.8): quicker with a player. Until now hevcdec only
noticed the block had finished a phase when the program next called it,
so while the program converted one picture the block finished the first
phase of the next and then sat idle, and the program waited for the
second phase afterwards. Now hevcdec looks between columns of every
conversion and starts the next phase at once. Pipelined, with the
pictures checked, "the program waited for the block" should now be
close to 0 cs (and its count, in brackets, close to 0), and "In all"
shorter than 0.1.8's. 10-bit rows are now unpacked straight into the
planes. Convert times the ways the copy could be done.

0.1.8 (hevcdec 0.1.7): 10-bit and 4K. For 10-bit streams the block
writes three samples to each 32-bit word (96 to a column's row instead
of 128); hevcdec unpacks them to 16-bit samples (FFmpeg's 10-bit
format), or to 8-bit (each sample's top 8 bits) for a player that shows
8-bit. hevctest checks the 16-bit pictures against FFmpeg's, and the
8-bit ones against the 16-bit ones. 4K (3840x2160) is the same decoder
with bigger frames: please run UHD as well, and say if it can't get the
memory.

0.1.6 (hevcdec 0.1.5): the block's two phases overlap. Phase 1 (reading
the bitstream) of the next picture runs while phase 2 (making the
picture) of the last does, and the program can carry on while the block
works, as a player would.
0.1.7 (hevcdec 0.1.6): the EMT trap, found. riscos-unixlib traced it
to the contiguous memory the block needs. RISC OS may hand over pages
that are in use by the running program, its page at &8000 included;
when that page is taken, RISC OS quietly copies the program to another
page. ARMEABISupport recognises each program by the physical address of
its page at &8000, and isn't told of the move, so it loses the program:
its stacks are never freed, and a later program that is given the old
page gets no stack handling and stops with the EMT trap. hevcdec now
never takes the page at &8000 (it asks for memory wholly below it or
wholly above it). Each run now ends with a line saying where the
program's page at &8000 was at the start and at the end; "MOVED" there
is a failure.
Please restart the Pi first, then run Test, Speed, Pipelined and
Repeat, then *ARMEABISupport_Info: it should list no programs (none of
these left a record behind). And please send Result, ResultT, ResultP
and ResultR.

If a run says a phase "didn't finish", restart before running anything
else; if the machine locks up, switch it off and on again.

Needs: a Raspberry Pi 4 (or 400) with RISC OS 5.23 or later (Physical
Memory Pools). 8-bit and 10-bit 4:2:0, up to 4096x4096.
Part of riscos-reelhwaccel; GPL version 2. hevcdec includes
Raspberry Pi's rpivid_h265.c (GPL version 2 or later; Copyright (C) 2020
Raspberry Pi (Trading) Ltd, based on the Cedrus VPU driver). The clips
are made from FFmpeg's testsrc2 pattern.
